 "method": "One revision measured in four separate passes, then compared against itself under all three balanced splits of those passes through bench/analyze-fixed-samples.js with --allow-identical-commits. Both sides of every split are the same binary, so each interval is this host's own spread. A workload's threshold is the widest end of the widest split, rounded up. All three are recorded because one split is one draw: two runs of this calibration at the same commit disagreed by a factor of eight on one workload, which is what a two-leg hierarchical bootstrap does. Every workload here is widened by leg variation rather than repetition variation, so more repetitions per leg would not tighten this lane -- more passes would. Only the straddling split can fail a calibration, because it is the arrangement the screening lane measures in and therefore the only one whose indistinguishability is being claimed; the other two are the same binary arranged so that session drift does not cancel, and a workload whose drift_splits is non-empty is one where that ordering is doing real work.",
